Let me analyze the bug in `fio_keyword_replace`:

```c
while ((s = strstr(opt, kw->word)) != NULL) {
        char *new = calloc(strlen(opt) + 1, 1);
        char *o_org = opt;
        int olen = s - opt;
        int len;

        memcpy(new, opt, olen);
        len = sprintf(new + olen, "%s", kw->replace);

        opt += strlen(kw->word) + olen;
        /* keeps final zero thanks to calloc */
        if (strlen(opt))
                memcpy(new + olen + len, opt, opt - o_org - 1);

        opt = new;
        free(o_org);
        docalc = 1;
}
```

The bug: `memcpy(new + olen + len, opt, opt - o_org - 1)` copies `opt - o_org - 1` bytes. But `opt - o_org = strlen(kw->word) + olen`, so it copies `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1` bytes from `opt`. The remaining string at `opt` has length `strlen(opt)`. If `strlen(opt) < strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1`, then memcpy reads beyond the buffer — a heap-buffer-overflow **read**.

Wait, but `opt` points into the original buffer `o_org` which has length `strlen(o_org)`. `opt` is at offset `strlen(kw->word) + olen` from `o_org`. The bytes available from `opt` to the end of the buffer is `strlen(o_org) - (strlen(kw->word) + olen)`. The memcpy reads `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1` bytes. So overflow read happens when `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1 > strlen(o_org) - (strlen(kw->word) + olen)`, i.e., `2*(strlen(kw->word) + olen) - 1 > strlen(o_org)`.

