`opt - o_org - 1 = strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1`. And `strlen(opt) = strlen(o_org) - (strlen(kw->word) + olen)`.

So overflow when `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1 > strlen(o_org) - strlen(kw->word) - olen`, i.e., `2*(strlen(kw->word)+olen) - 1 > strlen(o_org)`.

For `$ncpus` (6 chars), with `description=$ncpus` (17 chars): `olen = 12` (position of `$ncpus`), `strlen(kw->word)=6`. So `opt - o_org - 1 = 6+12-1 = 17`. `strlen(opt) = 17 - 17 = 0`. So memcpy reads 17 bytes from `opt` which points at the null terminator — reading 17 bytes past the end of the 17-byte buffer (well, the buffer is `strlen+1 = 18` bytes including null). `opt` points at index 17 (the null). Reading 17 bytes from index 17 = reading indices 17..33, but buffer is 18 bytes (0..17). So overflow read of 16 bytes past the end.

So the trigger is simply `description=$ncpus` (or any option with a keyword where the keyword is at the end). The description says exactly: "triggered when parsing options such as 'description=$ncpus'".

